// interleavings returns every merge of a and b that keeps each slice's own
// order: choose which positions the a records occupy.
func interleavings(a, b []string) [][]string {
	total := len(a) + len(b)
	var out [][]string
	var rec func(ai, bi int, prefix []string)
	rec = func(ai, bi int, prefix []string) {
		if ai == len(a) && bi == len(b) {
			merged := append([]string(nil), prefix...)
			out = append(out, merged)
			return
		}
		if ai < len(a) {
			rec(ai+1, bi, append(prefix, a[ai]))
		}
		if bi < len(b) {
			rec(ai, bi+1, append(prefix, b[bi]))
		}
	}
	rec(0, 0, nil)
	_ = total
	return out
}
```

Careful with append aliasing: append(prefix, ...) may share backing arrays between the two recursive branches. Since we copy at the leaf (`append([]string(nil), prefix...)`), sharing during construction is fine... Actually no: branch 1 does append(prefix, a[ai]) which may write into shared backing; then branch 2 append(prefix, b[bi]) overwrites the same slot — that's fine because each branch gets its own new slice header of len+1, and the leaf copies. The danger is only if a stored leaf shared backing with later writes — we copy at leaf. OK.

